# Fixed-format COBOL, not free-format

Every `.cob` file here is compiled with `cobc -x -std=cobol85`, which reads
fixed format. Column position is syntax. Getting it wrong is the subject of
the course, so do not emit free-format COBOL.

| Columns | Contents |
|---|---|
| 1-6 | Sequence number, ascending in hundreds (`000100`, `000200`) |
| 7 | Indicator: `*` comment, `-` continuation, blank for code |
| 8-11 | Area A: `DIVISION`, `SECTION`, paragraph names, `FD`, `01` levels |
| 12-72 | Area B: statements, and level numbers `02`-`49` |
| 73-80 | Identification, ignored by the compiler |

Nothing past column 72 is compiled, and drifting past it is silent.

```cobol
000100 IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
000900 FD POLICY-FILE.
001000 01 POLICY-REC.
001100     05 POL-NUM       PIC 9(6).
002000 MAIN-PARA.
002100     OPEN INPUT POLICY-FILE.
```

`FD` and `01` start in column 8. `05` and all statements start in column 12.

## Arithmetic

`ADD` and `MULTIPLY` take operands, not expressions, and `GIVING` assigns
rather than accumulates. To add to a running total, use `COMPUTE`.

```cobol
      * wrong: will not compile
003300     ADD CLAIMS * 50 TO NEW-PREM.
      * wrong: silently discards whatever NEW-PREM already held
003300     MULTIPLY CLAIMS BY 50 GIVING NEW-PREM.
      * right
003300     COMPUTE NEW-PREM = NEW-PREM + (CLAIMS * 50).
```

## File organization

Input decks are card images: fixed-length records with no line delimiters.
Leave `SELECT` statements without an `ORGANIZATION` clause so reads stay
record sequential. `LINE SEQUENTIAL` is a post-1985 extension and reading a
newline-delimited file as record sequential shifts every record after the
first one column left.
